            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>United Nations Partition Proposal</strong><br>\nIn 1947, the United Nations proposed dividing Palestine into a Jewish and an Arab state. This plan was accepted by Jewish leaders but rejected by Arab leaders. The partition plan led to the establishment of the State of Israel in 1948, but also to the beginning of the Arab-Israeli conflict.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Establishment of the State of Israel</strong><br>\nOn May 14, 1948, David Ben-Gurion, the Chairman of the Jewish National Council, declared Israel's independence. This led to a war between Israel and the surrounding Arab states. Despite major challenges, Israel was able to maintain its independence and began accepting Jewish immigrants from around the world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Palestine</strong><br>\nThe establishment of Israel had significant consequences for the Palestinian population. Many were displaced or fled and became refugees in the surrounding Arab countries. The West Bank and Gaza Strip came under Jordanian and Egyptian control, respectively.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
